  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Afro-Brazilian rural circle-dance tradition of the Vale do Paraíba (Rio-de-Janeiro and São-Paulo border, plantation-Bantu lineage 19th-century onward) characterized by call-and-response chant over candongueiro and tambu friction-drum patterns. Practiced by Kongo-Angolan-descended quilombo communities since colonial sugar-coffee-plantation labor, jongo nights feature umbigada-rotation-dance, riddle-and-spell sung-couplets (pontos), and trance-adjacent participatory circle structure. The tradition survived the post-abolition (1888-onward) rural-to-urban migration and remained the seedbed for early-20th-century samba-rural and samba-de-roda before the carioca-urban samba-canção codification.",
